<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhang Zuolin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhang Zuolin was a Chinese warlord who rose from bandit leader to master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> as head of the Fengtian clique. During the Beiyang period he fought the Zhili clique for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and from 1927 headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-government">Beiyang government</a> himself. Retreating before the Nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-expedition">Northern Expedition</a>, he was killed in June 1928 when Japanese officers blew up his train near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin.jpg" alt="Zhang Zuolin" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B. Nagakiya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhangzuolin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1928 the Kwantung Army assassinated the warlord <b>Zhang Zuolin</b>, hoping for anarchy as a pretext to seize the region; his son Zhang Xueliang, a Chinese nationalist, succeeded him instead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Yuan's failed attempt to restore the monarchy as the Empire of China (1915-1916) and his death, control of Beijing passed between military cliques: the Anhui clique of Duan Qirui, the Zhili clique of Cao Kun and Wu Peifu, and the Fengtian clique of <b>Zhang Zuolin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/beiyang-government">Beiyang government</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Haicheng, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Shenyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.85,122.75&amp;year=1909" aria-label="Haicheng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7556 705 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhang Zuolin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8006 975h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8006 975h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.85,122.75&amp;year=1909">What was here in 1909? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
